  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nUpper trough is moving through the area with southwest flow\nbringing light rain into the SE zones this afternoon, where temps\nare in the mid 40s. Flurries were obseved at the NWS office here\nwith temps around 30. Snow was falling in some northern zone\nlocations but observed accumulations have been less than an inch.\n\nUpper flow will become NW tonight as the trough axis shifts to our\neast. Flurries or snow showers will drift southeast across the\narea with little accumulation expected.\n\nUpper trough exits to our east tomorrow (Friday). Cold air \nremains over the area with NW flow aloft. After cold nights \ntonight and Friday night with lows in the teens and 20s. \nTemperatures will be somewhat warmer on Saturday as the coldest \nair moves to the east and stronger April sun begins a warming\ntrend.\n\nUpper ridge builds into the area starting on Sunday with dry and\nwarmer weather with temps peaking Wednesday in the 70s to low 80s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nPops were increased tonight slightly given the cyclonic upper NW\nflow. A model blend was modified with the placement. Later shifts\nmay have to modify this further.\n\nLeft the remaining grids alone. TFJ",
